e,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central mechanism accurately.\\n\\n**What it gets right:**\\n- When an ETF liquidates, shareholders receive their pro-rata share of net assets after liabilities and liquidation costs.\\n- It says liquidation does not ordinarily mean losing the whole holding, which distinguishes receiving remaining proceeds from total loss.\\n- It notes the payout is not necessarily the original investment or the latest quoted price.\\n- It explains the fund sells or disposes of its portfolio and pays shareholders based on the record date.\\n- It says timing varies and points to the fund's notice rather than assuming a fund-specific timetable.\\n- In a taxable account, it says the distribution triggers a gain or loss against adjusted cost basis, and fund-level gains may be passed through. This matches the One + One supplement and IRS Pub 550 principles.\\n\\n**Minor omissions (not material):**\\n- It does not explicitly say an investor can usually sell on the exchange before trading ends. It only implies this by noting trading may stop before cash is distributed.\\n- It does not say that recognizing a loss may wait until the final distribution, or that basis is recovered first with any excess taxed as capital gain. Measuring gain or loss against adjusted basis captures most of this.\\n\\nThere are no factual errors.\"}","ttft_ms":2923,"type":"result","duration_ms":6820,"uuid":"3f1cdfda-fc09-4c41-8e0d-aa201608344b","ttft_stream_ms":544,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":545,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
